```grf:context id="readme"
{
  "format": "grf.public-document/v1",
  "title": "README",
  "content": "GRF downstream 0.5.1\n\nAn MIT client for cooperative project checks, durable task sessions, evidence receipts, and authenticated cloud admission. Private service implementation and owner policies are not included.\n\nPython 3.11+. Open cloud signup uses the standard library only. Download the release ZIP, extract it, and verify its manifest with python verify_release.py.\n\nCreate a project and enroll automatically:\npython grf_starter.py init my-project --intent \"Build and verify my application\" --cloud\n\nNo invitation, operator approval, client certificate, Relay key, email or payment is required for this public plan. For an existing project created with this release:\npython grf_enroll.py my-project\n\nThen enter the project and follow QUICKSTART.grf. The initial acceptance check deliberately fails until replaced with a real application check. Successful signup is not a passing acceptance check.\n\nHow public cloud admission works\nEach project receives its own random identity and private bearer credential. The client generates the secret locally and sends only its SHA256 hash during signup. The server stores that hash, an enabled flag and credential expiry. On use, HTTPS carries the bearer credential, project identity and exact action hashes. No command text, source files, test output or local evidence is uploaded.\n\nThe cooperative public plan automatically registers users' own exact action hashes before requesting and consuming short-lived, single-use grants. It does not decide whether arbitrary commands are safe, independently evaluate local tests, or enforce private owner policy on public users. Each account is isolated from owner and other customer namespaces. Action registrations expire after two minutes; grants expire after ten seconds and do not survive service restart.\n\nCredentials stay in credentials/ with restrictive user permissions and a Git ignore entry. Do not share that directory. Renewal is automatic during use in the last seven days of the 30-day enrollment period. If unused until expired, create a new enrolled project; there is no email-based recovery. Existing unexpired enrollment can be renewed by rerunning grf_enroll.py. Operator revocation takes effect on subsequent API requests. A command already running is not remotely canceled by revocation.\n\nService limits\nInitial public capacity: 1,024 enrolled identities total, 256 recent action hashes per identity, a globally bounded service request rate, and a single small service replica. Busy responses are retried briefly; sustained outage or denial prevents launch. No availability SLA. Capacity is a launch limit, not an invitation requirement. Account state persists in Azure Table storage; commands and outputs are not retained by the application. Azure connection/control-plane metadata can exist. Action hashes are not encryption and predictable commands may be guessed.\n\nExisting manually enrolled mTLS/Relay clients remain supported. Their operator-approved action policy is separate and unchanged. Relay mode alone requires requirements-relay.txt.\n\nSecurity and licensing\nThis is a cooperative wrapper, not an OS sandbox or universal command interceptor. Someone controlling their PC can bypass or edit local checks. Windows job/POSIX group cleanup covers cooperative descendants; children share initial approval. Local receipts can contain test output; do not print secrets. A timeout is not rollback. No hostile-code, legal-compliance, full private-evaluator equivalence or patent-status claim is made.\n\nMIT covers distributed client files only; the private service and rules are excluded. Use only release artifacts from the verified publishing account and compare their separately published checksum. See HOSTED.grf and SECURITY.grf.\n"
}
```
